    "rationale": "Chose NO_FIX because the given scope (lines 170-180, the p_cycle_counter always block) contributes no combinational delay to this path. The path STARTS at the Q output of a cycle_counter bit (_124902_); the adder and FSM-state comparison inside my scope drive the D input, which is a different, non-violating cone. Reading the unattributed gates: cells 1-5 (NOR4/NAND4/OAI22/NOR2/AOI211, 0.31 ns) are the wide compare of cycle_counter against the bit-period constant that generates next_bit; cells 6-8 (AND3/AND3/NAND3, 0.31 ns, containing the 0.130 ns worst stage) qualify it with FSM state into the FIFO write strobe; cells 9-13 (three serial XOR2 plus NOR3, 0.24 ns) are the binary increment / binary-to-gray conversion in wptr_full closing on _125646_. RETIME rejected: the only register in scope is cycle_counter itself and it is the startpoint; it is also read by the FSM and sampling logic, so moving it across the compare (which I cannot see or edit) would change observable state, not just timing. RESTRUCTURE rejected: any rewrite of the increment or the fsm_state OR-chain only affects the D-side cone, which has slack, and cannot shorten the 0.62 ns compare/qualify chain downstream of Q; there is no expression on the violating path in this scope to flatten. PIPELINE rejected: inserting a register here would either delay next_bit by a cycle, stretching the UART bit period (a functional change to baud timing, not just latency), or demand matching re-balancing of next_bit, fsm_state and the FIFO write strobe, all of which lie outside the given lines - so no latency-neutral or safely-balanced cut exists within this window. A correct fix is to precompute the bit-period-elapsed condition one cycle early and register it, so next_bit emerges from a flop and the DFF-to-compare-to-qualify chain collapses; that edit lives in the next_bit generation logic of uart_rx.v above line 170, with the counter clear re-aligned accordingly. A lesser secondary gain is available by replacing the three-level serial XOR increment in wptr_full with a carry-lookahead increment, but at 0.24 ns it is not the dominant term.",
